<!-- ── GUIDE ── -->
<div id="screen-guide" class="screen">
  <div class="ph"><div class="ph-left"><h1>📖 How to Use This Planner</h1><p>Step-by-step guide to getting the most out of your family finance dashboard.</p></div></div>

  <div class="grid g2 mb2">
    <div class="card">
      <div class="guide-section">
        <h2>🚀 Getting Started</h2>
        <div class="guide-step"><div class="guide-num">1</div><div class="guide-step-body"><h3>Enter your income</h3><p>Go to <strong>Income</strong> and add every source of money coming in — salaries, rental income, dividends. Use <em>after-tax amounts</em> for the most accurate picture.</p></div></div>
        <div class="guide-step"><div class="guide-num">2</div><div class="guide-step-body"><h3>Log your expenses</h3><p>Go to <strong>Expenses</strong> and add all monthly costs. Break them into categories — housing, food, transport, school fees, etc. Include loan repayments here too if they're regular outgoings.</p></div></div>
        <div class="guide-step"><div class="guide-num">3</div><div class="guide-step-body"><h3>Add assets & debts</h3><p>In <strong>Assets</strong>, enter everything you own with its current value. In <strong>Debts</strong>, add outstanding balances with interest rates. This calculates your net worth.</p></div></div>
        <div class="guide-step"><div class="guide-num">4</div><div class="guide-step-body"><h3>Set goals & plans</h3><p>Use <strong>Savings Goals</strong> for targets (emergency fund, holiday). Use <strong>Future Plans</strong> for upcoming large purchases (car, renovation, school fees) so they appear in your forecast.</p></div></div>
        <div class="guide-step"><div class="guide-num">5</div><div class="guide-step-body"><h3>Explore your forecast</h3><p>Go to <strong>Forecast</strong> and move the sliders to model different scenarios. Try Conservative, Moderate, and Optimistic to understand your range of outcomes.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="guide-section">
        <h2>💡 Tips for Accuracy</h2>
        <div class="guide-tip"><strong>Use monthly amounts.</strong> All income and expense fields expect a monthly dollar figure. If you're paid fortnightly, multiply by 26 and divide by 12.</div>
        <div class="guide-tip"><strong>After-tax income is best.</strong> Enter what actually lands in your bank. This gives an accurate cashflow picture. You can note "before tax" in the Notes field if you prefer.</div>
        <div class="guide-tip"><strong>Super contributions in Assets.</strong> Add each person's superannuation as a separate asset with the "Superannuation" type. Enter the monthly employer contribution in the Contribution field.</div>
        <div class="guide-tip"><strong>Investment loan repayments.</strong> If you have an investment property loan, the repayment should go in <strong>Debts</strong> (not Expenses), so it flows correctly into the cashflow calculation.</div>
        <div class="guide-tip"><strong>Hover the ⓘ buttons.</strong> Every input field has a help button that explains exactly what to enter and gives an example.</div>
        <div class="guide-tip"><strong>Save to a specific location.</strong> Chrome, Edge, and Opera let you choose exactly where to save your plan file. Safari/Firefox save to Downloads. Use <strong>Save Plan</strong> on Overview.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card mb2">
    <div class="card-title">Key Concepts Explained</div>
    <div class="grid g2">
      <div>
        <div class="guide-faq-q">💳 What's the difference between Fixed, Variable, and Discretionary expenses?</div>
        <div class="guide-faq-a"><strong>Fixed:</strong> Same every month (mortgage, school fees, insurance). <strong>Variable:</strong> Changes but is necessary (groceries, petrol, utilities). <strong>Discretionary:</strong> Optional spending you could cut if needed (dining out, streaming services, hobbies).</div>
        <div class="guide-faq-q">📈 What annual return should I use for investments?</div>
        <div class="guide-faq-a">Australian shares historically average 7–10% p.a. Balanced superannuation funds average 6–8%. Property has averaged 5–7% in most Australian capital cities. Use the lower end for conservative planning.</div>
        <div class="guide-faq-q">🌅 What income growth rate should I enter?</div>
        <div class="guide-faq-a">Australia's average wage growth is around 3–4% per year. Use 2–3% for conservative, 4–5% for optimistic. If you expect a promotion or career change, you can model that separately in Future Plans.</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="guide-faq-q">🏠 Should I include my primary home as an asset?</div>
        <div class="guide-faq-a">Yes — it contributes to your net worth. Set the type to "Property (Primary)" and enter its estimated current market value. The mortgage goes in Debts. Note that you can't access this equity easily, so the planner treats it separately in the retirement simulation.</div>
        <div class="guide-faq-q">🏦 How does the Debt-to-Income ratio work?</div>
        <div class="guide-faq-a">DTI = total monthly loan repayments ÷ gross monthly income. Banks generally prefer this below 35–40%. Above 40% may limit your ability to borrow. The planner highlights this in red if it exceeds 35%.</div>
        <div class="guide-faq-q">🔮 How does the Forecast work?</div>
        <div class="guide-faq-a">It projects your net worth year by year using your current data as a starting point, then applies the growth rates and inflation from the sliders. Property grows at property rate, investments at investment rate, income at income rate, etc. Future Plans reduce your savings in the year they occur.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <div class="card-title">Save & Load Your Plan</div>
    <div class="grid g3">
      <div>
        <h3 class="fs12 fw6 mb1">💾 Saving to a Specific Location</h3>
        <p class="col2 fs11" style="line-height:1.7">
          Click <strong>Save Plan</strong> on the Overview page. In modern browsers (Chrome, Edge, Opera), you'll see a file picker where you can:
          <br>• Choose exactly where to save (e.g. next to this HTML file)
          <br>• Name your file (e.g. <code style="color:var(--sky)">family-finance-jan-2026.json</code>)
          <br>• Overwrite previous versions
          <br><br>Safari and Firefox: Files save to your Downloads folder by default.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="fs12 fw6 mb1">📂 Loading from Any Location</h3>
        <p class="col2 fs11" style="line-height:1.7">
          Click <strong>Load Plan</strong> on the Overview page. Browse to your saved <code style="color:var(--sky)">.json</code> file anywhere on your computer. All your data — income, expenses, assets, debts, goals, plans, and pension details — will be restored exactly as you saved it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="fs12 fw6 mb1">🔄 Best Practice: Monthly Updates</h3>
        <p class="col2 fs11" style="line-height:1.7">
          Each month: Load your plan → Update changed figures (pay rise, new expenses, asset values, super balance) → Save with a new date in the filename. This creates a monthly snapshot history and keeps your forecast accurate. Recommended naming: <code style="color:var(--sky)">finance-2026-01.json</code>, <code style="color:var(--sky)">finance-2026-02.json</code>, etc.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

